<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Águas Livres Aqueduct</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Aqueduto das Águas Livres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Águas Livres <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a> is a stone aqueduct that brought water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, begun in 1731 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> V and paid for largely by a tax on the city's residents. Its main arches over the Alcântara valley, completed by 1748, are among the tallest stone arches ever built. The structure survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a> and supplied the city until the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aguas-livres-aqueduct.jpg" alt="Águas Livres Aqueduct" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lijealso · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aqueduto_das_%C3%81guas_Livres2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
